# SPEC §8 "Registry safety": a change to a provider's allowed hosts, auth
# slots or denied paths decides where a key may be sent, so it needs a code
# owner's review. That covers the provider files and the multi-tenant suffix
# list, their compiled copy, the generator that writes it, the loader rules
# that check them, detection (which picks the provider whose hosts are
# copied into a new item), and the crate's tests that pin all of it.
#
# CODEOWNERS only names the reviewers. The two maintainer reviews SPEC §8
# asks for are branch protection settings on main, set by a repository
# admin: require a pull request, require review from code owners, and
# require 2 approving reviews. Setting them is an M1 release blocker
# (SPEC §14).
/providers/                                  @Mitsi-ag
/crates/envcloak-providers/src/embedded.rs   @Mitsi-ag
/crates/envcloak-providers/src/safety.rs     @Mitsi-ag
/crates/envcloak-providers/src/registry.rs   @Mitsi-ag
/crates/envcloak-providers/src/detect.rs     @Mitsi-ag
/crates/envcloak-providers/tests/            @Mitsi-ag
/scripts/gen-providers.py                    @Mitsi-ag
/.github/CODEOWNERS                          @Mitsi-ag

# SPEC §10a: the agent catalog decides which ancestor is an agent, and so
# the grant root and the agent barrier (§10b). A missed agent can ride a
# terminal grant, so the catalog, its compiled copy, the generator and the
# code that matches and roots with it need a code owner's review too.
/integrations/agents.toml                    @Mitsi-ag
/crates/envcloak-policy/src/agents_builtin.rs @Mitsi-ag
/crates/envcloak-policy/src/agents.rs        @Mitsi-ag
/crates/envcloak-policy/src/evidence.rs      @Mitsi-ag
/crates/envcloak-sys/src/proc/               @Mitsi-ag
/scripts/gen-agents.py                       @Mitsi-ag
# The Linux executable SHA-256 each ancestor's identity records (M2 plan
# D-09), which standing approvals will name (D-10).
/crates/envcloak-daemon/src/exe_hash.rs      @Mitsi-ag

# M2 plan D-02 (F-75): the crate graph is one-way, and a new edge between
# workspace crates is a plan change, never a table edit inside a feature
# task, so the allowed-edge table needs a code owner's review.
/scripts/check-crate-graph.py                @Mitsi-ag

# SPEC §6.8: the sign-in contract decides when a password is typed, where,
# and what session reaches the agent, and the driver parses every page the
# worker loads. Both crates need a code owner's review from the start
# (plan task M2-01).
/crates/envcloak-signin/                     @Mitsi-ag
/crates/envcloak-browser/                    @Mitsi-ag

# M2 plan D-23 and task M2-01: the reservation check's SHARED allowlist and
# its baseline of M1's entries decide which names may enter the code
# unreserved, and the SPEC check decides which decided sentences and
# security clauses must stay; like the crate-graph table, each needs a
# code owner's review.
/scripts/check-reservations.py               @Mitsi-ag
/scripts/check-reservations-baseline.txt     @Mitsi-ag
/scripts/check-spec-decisions.py             @Mitsi-ag
# The SPEC check catches the wordings and paraphrases reviews found; one it
# still misses is left to a code owner's review of the SPEC edit itself
# (check-spec-decisions.py, M2 plan §3), so the SPEC is code-owned too
# (task M3-01, whose security sentences rest on that review). Like the
# lines above, it takes effect once main's branch protection requires
# code-owner review.
/docs/SPEC.md                                @Mitsi-ag

# M3 plan D3-02, D3-05 and D3-06 (task M3-01): the macOS app, its build and
# signing scripts decide what is signed and with which entitlements, and
# the pinned requirements decide which signed code the daemon gives the
# `app` role and which daemon the clients trust (SPEC §4.3). Each needs a
# code owner's review from the first file.
/apps/macos/                                 @Mitsi-ag
/scripts/macos/                              @Mitsi-ag
# What the app compiles and ships from outside apps/macos (task M3-02): the
# brand's motion file (linked into EnvCloakDesign), icon and menu bar
# template; and the two scripts that decide whether the app's Swift checks
# cover what was compiled (check-sources.sh --swift) and whether the
# macos-app job runs on a pull request (ci-paths.py).
/assets/brand/                               @Mitsi-ag
/scripts/check-sources.sh                    @Mitsi-ag
/scripts/ci-paths.py                         @Mitsi-ag
/crates/envcloak-sys/src/peer_code.rs        @Mitsi-ag
/crates/envcloak-sys/src/peer_code/          @Mitsi-ag
